8
00:00:20.271 --> 00:00:35.801
How the grocery platform proves its services are correct before an outage does it for them, the test pyramid, property-based tests, consumer-driven contracts, linearizability checking, and integration tests against a real database with fault injection.

9
00:00:36.081 --> 00:00:39.793
Every lab is a real, runnable test that finds a real bug.

10
00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.015
L1: property-based testing.

11
00:00:42.295 --> 00:00:57.548
A hand-rolled property tester threw 1,000 random inputs at a money splitter, found that the naive one loses paise on odd splits, and shrank the failure to the minimal reproducer: total=1 split 2 ways loses 1 paisa.

12
00:00:58.898 --> 00:01:01.074
L2: consumer-driven contracts.

13
00:01:01.354 --> 00:01:17.077
A checkout consumer published a contract; verifying the provider against it (with no consumer running) passed v1 and failed v2 the instant the provider renamed totalpaise, then amount and dropped currency, naming the exact missing fields.

14
00:01:17.357 --> 00:01:19.511
L3: linearizability checking.

15
00:01:19.791 --> 00:01:32.975
A from-scratch checker (the idea behind Jepsen or Knossos) accepted a correct register history and rejected both a stale read and a lost update, with a concrete no valid order exists verdict.

16
00:01:34.325 --> 00:01:37.781
L4: integration testing on real PostgreSQL.

17
00:01:38.061 --> 00:01:53.251
A mock-free test proved isolation via rollback, caught a missing UNIQUE constraint (failed as shipped, passed after the migration), and verified the app fails cleanly when the DB backend is killed mid-transaction with no partial write.

18
00:01:53.531 --> 00:01:54.512
Reading time:

19
00:01:54.792 --> 00:01:56.755
120 min + code.

20
00:01:58.105 --> 00:01:58.958
Part 1.

21
00:01:59.238 --> 00:02:01.158
Introduction and Why It Matters.

22
00:02:02.408 --> 00:02:05.587
A story: the tests were green and the checkout was broken.

23
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:10.645
The order service had 94% unit-test coverage and a green pipeline.

24
00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:13.741
It still shipped three production incidents in one quarter:

25
00:02:14.021 --> 00:02:15.963
A rounding bug in split payments.

26
00:02:16.243 --> 00:02:26.739
When a shared cart's total didn't divide evenly across payers, the sum of the charges was one paisa short of the total, and the ledger reconciliation job failed every night.

27
00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:32.501
Every unit test passed, because every unit test used a total that happened to divide evenly.

28
00:02:32.781 --> 00:02:36.429
Nobody wrote the odd case, because nobody thought of it (L1).

29
00:02:37.779 --> 00:02:39.081
A broken contract.

30
00:02:39.361 --> 00:02:44.139
The cart team refactored a response field from totalpaise to amount and deployed.

31
00:02:44.419 --> 00:02:46.489
Their tests passed (they updated them).

32
00:02:46.769 --> 00:02:50.673
Checkout's tests passed (they mocked the cart response with the old shape).

33
00:02:50.953 --> 00:02:54.281
In production, checkout read null and every order failed.

34
00:02:54.561 --> 00:03:02.518
Two green pipelines, one outage, because the mock on the consumer side and the real provider had silently diverged (L2).

35
00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:05.660
A stale read from a replica.

36
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.591
Under load, order reads were routed to an async replica that lagged the primary.

37
00:03:10.871 --> 00:03:22.476
A customer added an item, and the next screen showed the old cart, a linearizability violation that no functional test caught, because it only appeared under concurrency + replica lag.

38
00:03:22.756 --> 00:03:29.391
The team argued for a week about whether it was really a bug before someone recorded a history and proved it (L3).

39
00:03:30.741 --> 00:03:36.351
The retro's conclusion: coverage measured how much code ran, not whether the system was correct.

40
00:03:36.631 --> 00:03:40.855
The mocks tested the team's assumptions about other services, not reality.

41
00:03:41.135 --> 00:03:53.381
And nothing tested the properties that actually matter in a distributed system, that money is conserved, that contracts hold across teams, that consistency guarantees are real under concurrency and failure.

42
00:03:53.661 --> 00:03:56.797
This chapter is about the tests that would have caught all three.

43
00:03:58.147 --> 00:04:00.451
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

