93
00:06:06.485 --> 00:06:07.360
Part 2.

94
00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:09.539
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

95
00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:14.458
Intuition: you can't test a distributed system by example.

96
00:06:14.738 --> 00:06:20.690
A single function has a manageable input space; a few examples plus the edge cases cover it.

97
00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:32.319
A distributed system's behaviour depends on inputs times interleavings times failures times timing, which is astronomically large and mostly invisible to example-based tests.

98
00:06:32.599 --> 00:06:40.834
So testing distributed systems is about changing the question: from does it work on these inputs? to does this property hold for all inputs?

99
00:06:41.114 --> 00:06:47.023
(property-based testing, L1);. from do my mocks match? to does the contract between services hold?

100
00:06:47.303 --> 00:06:49.074
(contract testing, L2);.

101
00:06:50.424 --> 00:06:55.309
from did the test pass? to does a valid explanation of this observed history exist?

102
00:06:55.589 --> 00:07:02.544
(linearizability, L3);. from does the mock behave? to does the real dependency behave, including when it fails?

103
00:07:02.824 --> 00:07:05.448
(integration + fault injection, L4).

104
00:07:06.798 --> 00:07:10.019
The test pyramid (and the ice-cream-cone anti-pattern).

105
00:07:10.299 --> 00:07:19.665
The pyramid: many fast, isolated unit tests at the base; fewer integration tests in the middle; a handful of slow end-to-end tests at the top.

106
00:07:19.945 --> 00:07:29.843
The shape follows from cost: unit tests are milliseconds and pinpoint failures; e2e tests are minutes, flaky, and only tell you something broke somewhere.

107
00:07:30.123 --> 00:07:41.195
The ice-cream cone (the anti-pattern) inverts it, mostly manual or e2e tests, few unit tests, and gives you slow, flaky pipelines that fail without saying why.

108
00:07:41.475 --> 00:07:56.793
In a microservices world the pyramid gains a crucial middle layer, contract tests (L2), which give integration-level confidence at unit-level speed by testing each side against a shared contract instead of spinning up the whole system.

109
00:07:58.143 --> 00:08:01.001
Test doubles: mocks, stubs, fakes, spies.

110
00:08:01.281 --> 00:08:03.884
A test double stands in for a real dependency:

111
00:08:04.164 --> 00:08:08.708
Stub, returns canned answers (when(repo.find(1)).thenReturn(order)).

112
00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:13.404
Mock, a stub that also asserts on interactions (was save() called once?).

113
00:08:13.684 --> 00:08:18.164
Fake, a working lightweight implementation (an in-memory repository).

114
00:08:18.444 --> 00:08:21.239
Spy, wraps a real object and records calls.

115
00:08:22.589 --> 00:08:28.669
Doubles make unit tests fast and deterministic, but they encode your assumptions about the dependency.

116
00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:38.591
The §1.1 contract break is the classic failure: the mock said the cart returns totalpaise, reality said amount, and the mock never updated.

117
00:08:38.871 --> 00:08:53.783
Rule of thumb: mock at architectural boundaries you own the contract for, and back every mocked cross-service boundary with a contract test (L2) and every mocked datastore with at least some integration tests against the real thing (L4).

118
00:08:55.133 --> 00:08:56.499
Property-based testing.

119
00:08:56.779 --> 00:09:07.445
Instead of examples, you state an invariant and let the framework generate hundreds of random inputs to try to break it; on failure it shrinks the input to the smallest counterexample.

120
00:09:07.725 --> 00:09:08.963
Classic properties:

121
00:09:09.243 --> 00:09:14.768
Round-trip: decode(encode(x)) == x (serializers, Ch 28).

122
00:09:15.048 --> 00:09:20.019
Invariant: the split parts sum to the total; a balance is never negative (L1).

123
00:09:20.299 --> 00:09:24.309
Oracle: the fast implementation agrees with a slow obvious one.

124
00:09:24.589 --> 00:09:29.965
Idempotency: f(f(x)) == f(x) (retries, Ch 20).

125
00:09:31.315 --> 00:09:37.609
Commutativity: applying events in any order yields the same state (CRDTs, Ch 12).

126
00:09:37.889 --> 00:09:43.670
Tools: jqwik (Java), Hypothesis (Python), QuickCheck (Haskell), fast-check (JS).

127
00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:50.286
L1 builds a tiny one from scratch, random generation + shrinking, and finds the paise-loss bug.

128
00:09:50.566 --> 00:10:07.867
Shrinking is the killer feature: a random failure at total=727563, n=9 is useless as a bug report; shrunk to total=1, n=2 it's obvious and becomes a one-line regression test.

129
00:10:09.217 --> 00:10:11.329
Contract testing (consumer-driven).

130
00:10:11.609 --> 00:10:25.263
In a consumer-driven contract (CDC), the consumer declares exactly the requests it makes and the response fields it depends on; that contract is published; the provider is verified against it in isolation.

131
00:10:25.543 --> 00:10:27.740
Neither side needs the other running.

132
00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:31.668
Why it beats end-to-end integration for cross-service correctness:

133
00:10:31.948 --> 00:10:36.449
Speed and stability: no need to stand up a fleet; each side tests alone.

134
00:10:36.729 --> 00:10:46.735
It catches the break at the source: the provider's build fails the moment it violates a contract a consumer relies on (L2), naming the fields, before deploy.

135
00:10:48.085 --> 00:11:02.399
It documents real usage: the contract is the consumer's actual needs, so the provider can freely change anything not in any contract (L2 changed items freely; it broke only because it touched totalpaise or currency).

136
00:11:02.679 --> 00:11:08.098
Pact is the common tool (a broker stores contracts and gates deploys via can-I-deploy).

137
00:11:08.378 --> 00:11:12.901
L2 builds the mechanic from scratch: publish, then verify, then break.

138
00:11:13.181 --> 00:11:23.997
CDC pairs naturally with schema-registry compatibility for events (Ch 28 L3), contracts for the sync API, the registry for the async bus.

139
00:11:25.347 --> 00:11:29.101
Integration testing with real dependencies (Testcontainers).

140
00:11:29.381 --> 00:11:42.843
Some bugs live only at the boundary with a real dependency: a constraint your ORM doesn't know about, a subtle SQL dialect difference, a transaction-isolation surprise, a serialization mismatch.

141
00:11:43.123 --> 00:11:55.176
Testcontainers starts a real PostgreSQL or Kafka or Redis in a throwaway Docker container per test run, so integration tests hit the real engine and clean up automatically.

142
00:11:55.456 --> 00:12:12.928
L4 uses a real PostgreSQL directly (Docker being unavailable here) and: isolates tests by running each in a transaction it rolls back (fast; the DB is pristine after), the alternative is schema or database-per-test (stronger isolation, slower);.

143
00:12:14.278 --> 00:12:26.203
catches a real schema bug a mock never could, a missing UNIQUE constraint (the mock enforces nothing);. injects a fault, killing the DB backend mid-transaction, to test the error path.

144
00:12:27.553 --> 00:12:31.009
Testing for consistency: linearizability and Jepsen.

145
00:12:31.289 --> 00:12:37.988
The hardest distributed bugs are consistency violations that appear only under concurrency + failure.

146
00:12:38.268 --> 00:12:47.527
You can't assert them with examples; you record a history (each operation's real-time window and result) and check whether a valid explanation exists.

147
00:12:47.807 --> 00:13:01.865
Linearizability (Ch 12): there is a single total order of operations consistent with real time (if A returns before B starts, A precedes B) and with the object's semantics (a read returns the last write).

148
00:13:02.145 --> 00:13:07.244
A checker searches for such an order; none existing = a violation (L3).

149
00:13:08.594 --> 00:13:28.605
Jepsen is the framework that does this at scale: it drives a real distributed database with randomized concurrent operations under injected faults (network partitions, process pauses, clock skew) via nemesis, records the history, and checks it with Knossos (linearizability) or Elle (transactional anomalies).

150
00:13:28.885 --> 00:13:32.341
It has found serious bugs in many production databases.

151
00:13:32.621 --> 00:13:39.639
L3 builds the checker's core, the real-time-aware search, and shows it rejecting a stale read and a lost update.

152
00:13:40.989 --> 00:13:42.973
Deterministic simulation testing.

153
00:13:43.253 --> 00:13:58.741
The most powerful technique for the implementation of a distributed system: run the entire system, every node, the network, the clock, disk, on a single-threaded deterministic simulation where you control time and message delivery from a seed.

154
00:13:59.021 --> 00:14:07.085
Because everything is deterministic, a failing seed replays exactly, so a one-in-a-billion race becomes a reproducible bug.

155
00:14:07.365 --> 00:14:12.677
You then inject partitions, reorderings, and crashes and explore millions of schedules.

156
00:14:12.957 --> 00:14:19.677
FoundationDB pioneered this; TigerBeetle, and frameworks like madsim (Rust) and turmoil follow it.

157
00:14:19.957 --> 00:14:33.632
It requires designing the code for it (all I or O and time behind injectable interfaces), which is a significant commitment, the payoff is finding and reproducing concurrency bugs that are otherwise Heisenbugs.

158
00:14:34.982 --> 00:14:37.286
End-to-end, smoke, and canary tests.

159
00:14:37.566 --> 00:14:45.075
End-to-end exercises a full user journey (add to cart, then checkout, then order confirmed) across real services.

160
00:14:45.355 --> 00:14:50.177
Keep them few, they're slow and flaky, and reserve them for the critical revenue paths.

161
00:14:50.457 --> 00:14:58.499
Smoke tests are a tiny set run right after deploy to confirm the system is alive (health endpoints, one login, one order).

162
00:14:58.779 --> 00:15:10.171
Canary or synthetic monitoring runs scripted journeys continuously in production against real endpoints, alerting when a journey breaks, testing in production (Ch 24).

163
00:15:11.521 --> 00:15:13.740
Flaky tests, test data, and CI.

164
00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:20.740
Flaky tests (pass or fail non-deterministically) destroy trust in the suite, people start ignoring red.

165
00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:27.420
Causes: timing or sleep, shared state between tests, real time or clock, ordering dependence, network.

166
00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:39.263
Fixes: control time (inject a clock), isolate state (L4's rollback), await conditions instead of sleeping, quarantine and fix, never retry-until-green as a habit.

167
00:15:39.543 --> 00:15:47.884
Test data: builders or factories over shared fixtures; each test creates what it needs; reset between tests (rollback or truncate).

168
00:15:49.234 --> 00:16:01.863
CI: run the fast layers on every push, gate merges on green, run slow integration or e2e on a schedule or pre-merge; make the pipeline fast (parallelize) or people route around it.

169
00:16:03.213 --> 00:16:06.392
Coverage, mutation testing, and what tested means.

170
00:16:06.672 --> 00:16:14.501
Line or branch coverage measures which code ran, not whether it was checked, you can have 100% coverage with no assertions.

171
00:16:14.781 --> 00:16:29.032
Mutation testing (PIT for Java) measures test quality: it makes small changes to your code (mutations) and checks whether a test fails; a surviving mutant is code your tests execute but don't actually verify.

172
00:16:29.312 --> 00:16:41.323
It's the honest answer to are these tests worth anything? far more meaningful than a coverage percentage, and the antidote to the §1.1 94% coverage, still broken trap.

173
00:16:42.673 --> 00:16:43.569
Part 3.

