260
00:23:49.871 --> 00:24:04.441
PROPERTY-BASED TESTING from scratch: instead of a few hand-picked examples, we state a PROPERTY that must hold for ALL. inputs and let the framework throw hundreds of random inputs at it, then SHRINK any failure to the smallest counter-.

261
00:24:07.291 --> 00:24:08.209
On screen:

262
00:24:08.489 --> 00:24:16.723
Property-based testing: state one property ('the split parts sum to the total') and. throw 1000 random. inputs at each splitter.

263
00:24:17.003 --> 00:24:21.633
A failure is SHRUNK to the smallest input that still breaks. it. splitNaive:

264
00:24:21.913 --> 00:24:22.723
FAILED.

265
00:24:23.003 --> 00:24:39.686
Random counterexample total=727563 n=9; shrunk to the minimal. failing case: total=1 split 2 ways = 0, 0, then sum=0, LOST 1 paise. splitCorrect:

266
00:24:39.966 --> 00:24:59.337
PASSED 1000 random cases, property 'parts sum to total' holds. then the naive splitter passes hand-picked even cases (100 or 4 = 25 each) but a random. search finds an ODD. case in milliseconds; shrinking reports the minimal reproducer (for example, 1 paisa.

267
00:24:59.617 --> 00:25:01.131
The rest is in the chapter.

268
00:25:03.981 --> 00:25:25.635
Reading it: splitNaive (integer division) passes hand-picked even cases but a random search finds an odd one immediately; shrinking reduces the messy total=727563, n=9 to the minimal total=1, n=2, then parts 0,0, 1 paisa lost.

269
00:25:25.915 --> 00:25:35.536
That minimal case is the regression unit test you commit. splitCorrect distributes the remainder and passes all 1,000 cases, the property holds.

270
00:25:36.886 --> 00:25:48.897
The lesson: example-based tests only cover the cases you thought of; a property + random generation covers the ones you didn't, and shrinking makes every failure a clean, minimal bug report.

271
00:25:49.177 --> 00:26:03.811
In real code you'd use jqwik (@Property void partssumtototal(@ForAll long total, @ForAll @IntRange(min=1,max=20) int n)), which generates, shrinks and re-runs the shrunk case automatically.

