00:28:49.381 --> 00:28:53.242
L3, Linearizability checking (the idea behind Jepsen).

300
00:28:53.522 --> 00:28:54.461
Requirement:

301
00:28:54.741 --> 00:28:57.258
Under load, a read returned a stale cart.

302
00:28:57.538 --> 00:29:00.823
The team argued for a week about whether it was 'really' a bug.

303
00:29:01.103 --> 00:29:02.341
Prove it mechanically.

304
00:29:02.621 --> 00:29:16.487
A from-scratch checker takes a recorded history (each operation's real-time window invoke, return and result) and searches for a single sequential order that respects both real time and register semantics.

305
00:29:16.767 --> 00:29:21.845
If none exists, the history is not linearizable, the system violated its guarantee.

306
00:29:23.195 --> 00:29:26.032
Here is the Java code, LinearL3.java.

307
00:29:26.312 --> 00:29:30.493
It runs to 122 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

308
00:29:30.773 --> 00:29:37.643
LinearL3.java, run with: java LinearL3.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

309
00:29:37.923 --> 00:29:41.912
LINEARIZABILITY CHECKING, the technique behind Jepsen or Knossos.

310
00:29:42.192 --> 00:29:53.179
You cannot unit-test a distributed register by asking. is it correct?; you RECORD a concurrent HISTORY of operations (each with the real-time window invoke, return and.

311
00:29:56.029 --> 00:29:56.946
On screen:

312
00:29:57.226 --> 00:30:06.527
Linearizability checking: given a recorded history of a distributed register (initial. value 0), does a valid. sequential order exist?

313
00:30:06.807 --> 00:30:12.546
The checker respects real-time order AND register semantics (a. read = the last write).

314
00:30:12.826 --> 00:30:16.154
History 1, correct register (quorum reads or writes):

315
00:30:16.434 --> 00:30:18.589
C1 write(1) 0..3.

316
00:30:18.869 --> 00:30:21.557
C2 read()-1 2..5.

317
00:30:21.837 --> 00:30:23.927
C1 write(2) 6..8.

318
00:30:24.207 --> 00:30:28.218
C3 read()-2 9..11. then LINEARIZABLE.

319
00:30:28.498 --> 00:30:30.567
A valid real-time order exists:

320
00:30:30.847 --> 00:30:33.258
W1 R1 W2 R2.

321
00:30:33.538 --> 00:30:36.461
History 2, async replica served a STALE read:

322
00:30:36.741 --> 00:30:38.255
The rest is in the chapter.

323
00:30:41.105 --> 00:30:41.959
Reading it:

324
00:30:42.239 --> 00:30:54.996
History 1 (correct register) is linearizable, the checker even prints a valid order (W1 R1 W2 R2); C2's read overlapped C1's write, so returning 1 is legal.

325
00:30:55.276 --> 00:31:05.772
History 2 (async replica) is rejected: write(1) completed and a read observed 1, then a later read returned 0, the value travelled backwards in time.

326
00:31:06.052 --> 00:31:09.231
No order can respect both real time and semantics.

327
00:31:10.581 --> 00:31:20.863
History 3 (lost update) is rejected: two writes completed in sequence (1 then 2), but a later read returned 1, it must see the last completed write, 2.

328
00:31:21.143 --> 00:31:24.045
This is the checker at the heart of Jepsen or Knossos.

329
00:31:24.325 --> 00:31:37.637
The other half is generating randomized concurrent histories under a nemesis (partitions, pauses, clock skew) and checking each, which is how Jepsen finds violations that survive every functional test.

330
00:31:37.917 --> 00:31:43.037
Real checkers (Knossos, Elle) add heavy pruning because the search is exponential.

331
00:31:44.387 --> 00:31:49.421
L4, Integration testing on real PostgreSQL, with fault injection.

332
00:31:49.701 --> 00:31:50.640
Requirement:

333
00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:55.741
A mocked repository passed while a missing DB constraint let duplicates through.

334
00:31:56.021 --> 00:31:59.157
Test against the real database, including when it fails.

335
00:31:59.437 --> 00:32:13.837
This runs against real PostgreSQL 16 and does three things a mock cannot: isolate tests via rollback, catch a missing UNIQUE constraint, and inject a real fault by killing the DB backend mid-transaction.

336
00:32:15.187 --> 00:32:18.515
Here is the Java code, IntegrationL4.java.

337
00:32:18.795 --> 00:32:23.126
It runs to 142 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

338
00:32:23.406 --> 00:32:37.294
IntegrationL4.java, run with: java -cp or tmp or pgjdbc.jar IntegrationL4.java (real PostgreSQL 16 on:5581).

339
00:32:37.574 --> 00:32:47.729
INTEGRATION TESTING against a REAL database (what Testcontainers gives you when Docker is available, here we use a. real local PostgreSQL directly).

340
00:32:48.009 --> 00:32:51.550
Three things unit tests with a mocked repository CANNOT do:

341
00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:55.317
On screen:

342
00:32:55.597 --> 00:33:00.141
Integration testing against REAL PostgreSQL 16 (not a mock).

343
00:33:00.421 --> 00:33:02.853
Three capabilities a mock. can't give you:

344
00:33:03.133 --> 00:33:09.789
A) Isolation: each test runs in a transaction that is rolled back, the table is. untouched afterward.

345
00:33:10.069 --> 00:33:16.469
PASS 5 tests each inserted a row then rolled back; row count unchanged (0 ==.

346
00:33:16.749 --> 00:33:17.539
0).

347
00:33:17.819 --> 00:33:19.717
B) Catch a real schema bug:

348
00:33:19.997 --> 00:33:22.557
'registering the same email twice must fail'.

349
00:33:22.837 --> 00:33:26.848
Run 1, against the table AS SHIPPED (no unique constraint on email):

350
00:33:27.128 --> 00:33:29.432
FAIL duplicate email is rejected.

351
00:33:29.712 --> 00:33:32.784
Run 2, after the migration adds UNIQUE (email):

352
00:33:33.064 --> 00:33:35.240
PASS duplicate email is rejected.

353
00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:37.035
The rest is in the chapter.

354
00:33:39.885 --> 00:33:40.738
Reading it:

355
00:33:41.018 --> 00:33:49.722
A, Isolation: five tests each insert a row and roll back; the row count is unchanged afterward, so tests can't pollute each other.

356
00:33:50.002 --> 00:33:52.605
Fast, no cleanup, no ordering dependence.

357
00:33:53.955 --> 00:34:06.307
B, Real bug caught: duplicate email must be rejected FAILS against the table as shipped (no UNIQUE constraint, both inserts succeed) and PASSES after the migration adds UNIQUE (email).

358
00:34:06.587 --> 00:34:10.768
A mock enforces nothing, so it would have passed both times and hidden the bug.

359
00:34:11.048 --> 00:34:17.107
(The suite's 1 failed is the caught bug, exactly what you want the test to do before the constraint exists.).

360
00:34:18.457 --> 00:34:34.222
C, Fault injection: we pgterminatebackend the very connection mid-transaction; the commit() throws (terminating connection due to administrator command) and no partial row survives, the app fails cleanly and the uncommitted write is discarded.

361
00:34:34.502 --> 00:34:38.427
This exercises the error path that only a real failure triggers.

362
00:34:38.707 --> 00:34:40.051
Framework equivalent:

