364
00:34:52.988 --> 00:34:53.927
Part 6.

365
00:34:54.207 --> 00:34:55.487
Production Concerns.

366
00:34:56.737 --> 00:34:58.465
Failure modes of a test suite.

367
00:34:58.745 --> 00:35:00.110
Here is a quick table:

368
00:35:00.390 --> 00:35:02.587
Failure, and Cause, and Mitigation.

369
00:35:02.867 --> 00:35:04.702
Green pipeline, broken prod.

370
00:35:04.982 --> 00:35:05.750
Cause:

371
00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:09.550
Coverage without assertions; mocks that don't match reality.

372
00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:10.833
Mitigation:

373
00:35:11.113 --> 00:35:18.345
Mutation testing (§2.11); contract tests (L2); integration on real deps (L4).

374
00:35:18.625 --> 00:35:19.606
Mock drift.

375
00:35:19.886 --> 00:35:20.654
Cause:

376
00:35:20.934 --> 00:35:23.622
Consumer mock and provider diverge silently.

377
00:35:23.902 --> 00:35:24.905
Mitigation:

378
00:35:25.185 --> 00:35:27.958
Consumer-driven contracts gating deploys (L2).

379
00:35:28.238 --> 00:35:29.539
Missed edge cases.

380
00:35:29.819 --> 00:35:30.587
Cause:

381
00:35:30.867 --> 00:35:32.574
Only example-based tests.

382
00:35:32.854 --> 00:35:33.857
Mitigation:

383
00:35:34.137 --> 00:35:37.230
Property-based tests for invariant logic (L1).

384
00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:39.003
Consistency bug shipped.

385
00:35:39.283 --> 00:35:40.051
Cause:

386
00:35:40.331 --> 00:35:43.190
No test exercises concurrency + faults.

387
00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:44.473
Mitigation:

388
00:35:44.753 --> 00:35:50.875
Linearizability or Jepsen checks (L3); deterministic simulation (§2.8).

389
00:35:51.155 --> 00:35:52.542
Flaky suite ignored.

390
00:35:52.822 --> 00:35:53.590
Cause:

391
00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:55.939
Timing, shared state, real clock.

392
00:35:56.219 --> 00:35:57.222
Mitigation:

393
00:35:57.502 --> 00:36:03.795
Control time, isolate state (L4), await not sleep, quarantine (§2.10).

394
00:36:05.145 --> 00:36:06.745
Slow suite routed around.

395
00:36:07.025 --> 00:36:07.793
Cause:

396
00:36:08.073 --> 00:36:10.377
Ice-cream cone; serial e2e.

397
00:36:10.657 --> 00:36:11.660
Mitigation:

398
00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:16.100
Rebalance to the pyramid; parallelize; contracts over e2e.

399
00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:17.596
Works on my machine.

400
00:36:17.876 --> 00:36:18.644
Cause:

401
00:36:18.924 --> 00:36:21.313
Tests depend on local state or ordering.

402
00:36:21.593 --> 00:36:22.596
Mitigation:

403
00:36:22.876 --> 00:36:26.247
Hermetic tests; ephemeral real deps (Testcontainers).

404
00:36:27.597 --> 00:36:30.413
Observability of the suite (Ch 24).

405
00:36:30.693 --> 00:36:39.247
Track flaky rate per test, suite duration and its trend, mutation score on changed code, and contract-verification results per pair.

406
00:36:39.527 --> 00:36:47.463
In production, synthetic or canary journeys and SLO monitoring are the continuation of testing, they catch what pre-prod can't.

407
00:36:48.813 --> 00:36:49.752
Security.

408
00:36:50.032 --> 00:36:57.243
Tests must not use production data with real PII; use synthetic or anonymized data (Ch 23).

409
00:36:57.523 --> 00:37:04.627
Never bake real secrets into test fixtures or CI logs; use a test vault or rotated test credentials.

410
00:37:04.907 --> 00:37:13.952
Include security tests: authz checks (a user can't read another's orders), input validation, and dependency or SAST scans in CI.

411
00:37:15.302 --> 00:37:17.115
Performance and scale of testing.

412
00:37:17.395 --> 00:37:25.374
Parallelize unit or property tests (they're independent); shard integration tests; cache dependencies and container images.

413
00:37:25.654 --> 00:37:30.305
Property tests: tune case count and generators (bias toward boundaries:

414
00:37:30.585 --> 00:37:38.841
0, 1, max, negatives); use a fixed seed in CI for reproducibility and a random seed nightly to keep exploring.

415
00:37:39.121 --> 00:37:46.993
Reuse a single Testcontainer across a class where isolation allows (rollback-per-test), rather than a fresh container per test.

416
00:37:48.343 --> 00:37:49.068
Cost.

417
00:37:49.348 --> 00:37:56.985
The expensive layers are integration or e2e (compute, time) and Jepsen or simulation runs (long, scheduled).

418
00:37:57.265 --> 00:38:05.799
Spend there proportionally to risk: payments and inventory get consistency and fault-injection testing; a static content service does not.

419
00:38:06.079 --> 00:38:13.247
The cheap layers (unit or property or contract) are where most confidence should come from, that's the whole point of the pyramid.

420
00:38:14.597 --> 00:38:15.535
Part 7.

421
00:38:15.815 --> 00:38:17.522
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

422
00:38:18.772 --> 00:38:19.689
Watch out:

423
00:38:19.969 --> 00:38:20.609
1.

424
00:38:20.889 --> 00:38:22.404
Chasing a coverage number.

425
00:38:22.684 --> 00:38:28.231
100% line coverage with weak assertions catches nothing (§1.1).

426
00:38:28.511 --> 00:38:32.500
Measure test quality with mutation testing, not lines executed.

427
00:38:33.850 --> 00:38:34.767
Watch out:

428
00:38:35.047 --> 00:38:35.751
2.

429
00:38:36.031 --> 00:38:37.290
The ice-cream cone.

430
00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:44.845
Mostly manual or e2e tests, few unit tests, then slow, flaky pipelines that can't localize failures.

431
00:38:45.125 --> 00:38:46.725
Invert it back to the pyramid.

432
00:38:48.075 --> 00:38:48.992
Watch out:

433
00:38:49.272 --> 00:38:50.019
3.

434
00:38:50.299 --> 00:38:53.456
Mocking a cross-service boundary with no contract test.

435
00:38:53.736 --> 00:38:56.595
Your mock encodes assumptions that silently rot.

436
00:38:56.875 --> 00:39:01.376
Back every mocked service boundary with a consumer-driven contract (L2).

437
00:39:02.726 --> 00:39:03.643
Watch out:

438
00:39:03.923 --> 00:39:04.627
4.

439
00:39:04.907 --> 00:39:07.830
Only example-based tests for invariant logic.

440
00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:11.566
You test the cases you thought of; the bug is in the case you didn't.

441
00:39:11.846 --> 00:39:16.667
Use property-based tests for money, serialization, idempotency (L1).

442
00:39:18.017 --> 00:39:18.935
Watch out:

443
00:39:19.215 --> 00:39:19.983
5.

444
00:39:20.263 --> 00:39:22.823
Asserting consistency with functional tests.

445
00:39:23.103 --> 00:39:27.220
Strong-consistency bugs only appear under concurrency + faults.

446
00:39:27.500 --> 00:39:32.321
You must record a history and check it (L3), not eyeball a single-threaded test.

447
00:39:33.671 --> 00:39:34.589
Watch out:

448
00:39:34.869 --> 00:39:35.615
6.

449
00:39:35.895 --> 00:39:39.095
Testing only against an in-memory fake of your database.

450
00:39:39.375 --> 00:39:48.122
H2-standing-in-for-Postgres hides constraint, dialect, and isolation bugs (L4's missing UNIQUE would pass against a naive fake).

451
00:39:48.402 --> 00:39:50.493
Integration-test the real engine.

452
00:39:51.843 --> 00:39:52.760
Watch out:

453
00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:53.787
7.

454
00:39:54.067 --> 00:39:55.475
Thread.sleep in tests.

455
00:39:55.755 --> 00:39:58.549
Timing-based waits are the 1 source of flakiness.

456
00:39:58.829 --> 00:40:03.267
Await a condition (poll with a deadline) or inject a controllable clock.

457
00:40:04.617 --> 00:40:05.534
Watch out:

458
00:40:05.814 --> 00:40:06.497
8.

459
00:40:06.777 --> 00:40:08.590
Retry-until-green in CI.

460
00:40:08.870 --> 00:40:14.097
Auto-retrying flaky tests hides real intermittent bugs and normalizes red.

461
00:40:14.377 --> 00:40:16.489
Quarantine and fix the flake instead.

462
00:40:17.839 --> 00:40:18.756
Watch out:

463
00:40:19.036 --> 00:40:19.783
9.

464
00:40:20.063 --> 00:40:22.025
Shared mutable state between tests.

465
00:40:22.305 --> 00:40:26.295
Order-dependent tests pass alone and fail in the suite (or vice versa).

466
00:40:26.575 --> 00:40:30.393
Isolate: rollback (L4), fresh fixtures, no static state.

467
00:40:31.743 --> 00:40:32.661
Watch out:

468
00:40:32.941 --> 00:40:33.645
10.

469
00:40:33.925 --> 00:40:35.567
No fault-injection tests.

470
00:40:35.847 --> 00:40:41.053
Happy-path integration tests never exercise the error handling that matters most.

471
00:40:41.333 --> 00:40:48.629
Kill connections, add latency, return 500s, and assert clean failure (L4-C, Ch 27).

472
00:40:49.979 --> 00:40:50.896
Watch out:

473
00:40:51.176 --> 00:40:52.008
11.

474
00:40:52.288 --> 00:41:05.600
A random counterexample with no shrinking. total=727563, n=9 is a useless bug report; total=1, n=2 is obvious.

475
00:41:05.880 --> 00:41:10.083
Always shrink, and commit the shrunk case as a regression test (L1).

476
00:41:11.433 --> 00:41:12.350
Watch out:

477
00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:13.419
12.

478
00:41:13.699 --> 00:41:16.942
Testing in prod as a substitute for pre-prod tests.

479
00:41:17.222 --> 00:41:21.019
Canaries and synthetics complement the pyramid; they don't replace it.

480
00:41:21.299 --> 00:41:22.131
Do both.

481
00:41:23.481 --> 00:41:24.356
Part 8.

482
00:41:24.636 --> 00:41:25.980
Interview Q and A.

483
00:41:27.230 --> 00:41:28.062
Beginner.

484
00:41:28.342 --> 00:41:29.366
Question 1.

485
00:41:29.646 --> 00:41:32.057
What is the test pyramid, and why that shape?

486
00:41:32.337 --> 00:41:33.702
Thirty-second answer:

487
00:41:33.982 --> 00:41:41.982
Many fast, isolated unit tests at the base; fewer integration tests in the middle; a few slow end-to-end tests at the top.

488
00:41:42.262 --> 00:41:52.246
The shape follows cost and signal: unit tests are milliseconds and pinpoint the failure; e2e tests are slow, flaky, and only say something broke.

489
00:41:52.526 --> 00:41:55.918
You want most confidence to come from the cheap, precise layers.

490
00:41:56.198 --> 00:42:01.105
The inverted version, mostly e2e, is the ice-cream cone anti-pattern.

491
00:42:02.455 --> 00:42:03.457
Question 2.

492
00:42:03.737 --> 00:42:07.065
Mocks versus stubs versus fakes, and the risk of mocking?

493
00:42:07.345 --> 00:42:08.711
Thirty-second answer:

494
00:42:08.991 --> 00:42:22.559
A stub returns canned values; a mock also asserts on interactions (was this called?); a fake is a working lightweight implementation (in-memory repo); a spy wraps a real object and records calls.

495
00:42:22.839 --> 00:42:35.212
The risk: a double encodes your assumption about the dependency, which can silently diverge from reality, a mocked service response with the wrong field shape passes your test and breaks in production.

496
00:42:36.562 --> 00:42:37.607
Question 3.

497
00:42:37.887 --> 00:42:41.770
What's the difference between unit, integration, and end-to-end tests?

498
00:42:42.050 --> 00:42:43.415
Thirty-second answer:

499
00:42:43.695 --> 00:42:48.538
Unit tests one function or class in isolation with doubles (fast, thousands).

500
00:42:48.818 --> 00:42:56.498
Integration tests your code with a real dependency, a database, a broker, to catch boundary bugs (slower, hundreds).

501
00:42:56.778 --> 00:43:02.239
End-to-end tests a full user journey across multiple real services (slowest, a few).

502
00:43:02.519 --> 00:43:06.594
Each proves something different; you need all three in pyramid proportions.

503
00:43:07.944 --> 00:43:09.011
Question 4.

504
00:43:09.291 --> 00:43:11.723
What makes a test flaky, and why does it matter?

505
00:43:12.003 --> 00:43:13.368
Thirty-second answer:

506
00:43:13.648 --> 00:43:24.485
A flaky test passes and fails without the code changing, usually from timing (sleep), shared state between tests, real clock or time, test-ordering dependence, or network.

507
00:43:24.765 --> 00:43:32.979
It matters because it destroys trust: once people see red that isn't real, they start ignoring red, and a genuine failure slips through.

508
00:43:33.259 --> 00:43:35.392
Fix flakes; don't retry them away.

509
00:43:36.742 --> 00:43:37.745
Intermediate.

510
00:43:38.025 --> 00:43:39.134
Question 5.

511
00:43:39.414 --> 00:43:42.422
What is property-based testing and when do you reach for it?

512
00:43:42.702 --> 00:43:44.067
Thirty-second answer:

513
00:43:44.347 --> 00:43:56.251
Instead of hand-picked examples, you state an invariant that must hold for all inputs and let the framework throw hundreds of random inputs at it, shrinking any failure to the minimal counterexample.

514
00:43:56.531 --> 00:44:08.350
Reach for it when a property is easy to state but the input space is large: money math, serialization round-trips, idempotency, ordering-independence, or a fast-versus-reference oracle.

515
00:44:09.700 --> 00:44:10.809
Question 6.

516
00:44:11.089 --> 00:44:13.479
Explain consumer-driven contract testing.

517
00:44:13.759 --> 00:44:15.124
Thirty-second answer:

518
00:44:15.404 --> 00:44:27.329
The consumer declares exactly the requests it makes and the response fields it depends on; that contract is published; the provider is verified against it in isolation, with no consumer running.

519
00:44:27.609 --> 00:44:33.817
If the provider changes something a consumer relies on, its build fails immediately, naming the fields.

520
00:44:34.097 --> 00:44:39.559
It gives cross-service correctness at unit-test speed and lets teams deploy independently.

521
00:44:40.909 --> 00:44:41.997
Question 7.

522
00:44:42.277 --> 00:44:46.095
Why test against a real database instead of an in-memory fake?

523
00:44:46.375 --> 00:44:47.741
Thirty-second answer:

524
00:44:48.021 --> 00:44:59.242
Because the bugs that matter at the DB boundary, constraints, unique or foreign keys, SQL-dialect quirks, transaction isolation, sequence behaviour, don't exist in a fake.

525
00:44:59.522 --> 00:45:05.495
An H2-for-Postgres fake will happily accept a duplicate the real UNIQUE index would reject.

526
00:45:05.775 --> 00:45:11.087
Testcontainers spins up a real engine per run so integration tests hit reality.

527
00:45:12.437 --> 00:45:13.461
Question 8.

528
00:45:13.741 --> 00:45:17.283
How do you keep integration tests isolated and repeatable?

529
00:45:17.563 --> 00:45:18.928
Thirty-second answer:

530
00:45:19.208 --> 00:45:28.147
Each test starts a transaction, does its work, asserts, and rolls back, so the database is pristine for the next test and tests can't pollute each other.

531
00:45:28.427 --> 00:45:30.219
It's fast and needs no cleanup.

532
00:45:30.499 --> 00:45:39.160
Where a test must commit (to test triggers, or cross-connection visibility), use schema-per-test or truncate-between, which are stronger but slower.

533
00:45:40.510 --> 00:45:41.577
Question 9.

534
00:45:41.857 --> 00:45:45.121
What is mutation testing and why prefer it to coverage?

535
00:45:45.401 --> 00:45:46.766
Thirty-second answer:

536
00:45:47.046 --> 00:45:54.406
Mutation testing makes tiny changes to your code (flip a to =, remove a line) and checks whether any test fails.

537
00:45:54.686 --> 00:46:00.659
A surviving mutant is code your tests run but don't actually verify, a gap coverage can't see.

538
00:46:00.939 --> 00:46:09.345
It measures test quality, whereas line coverage only measures which code executed, which can be 100% with zero assertions.

539
00:46:10.695 --> 00:46:11.463
Senior.

540
00:46:11.743 --> 00:46:12.788
Question 10.

541
00:46:13.068 --> 00:46:15.841
What is linearizability and how do you test for it?

542
00:46:16.121 --> 00:46:17.487
Thirty-second answer:

543
00:46:17.767 --> 00:46:31.975
Linearizability means every operation appears to take effect atomically at some instant between its invocation and its response, consistent with real time and the object's semantics (a read returns the latest write).

544
00:46:32.255 --> 00:46:44.180
You can't assert it by example; you record a history, each op's real-time window and result, and run a checker that searches for a single sequential order respecting real time and semantics.

545
00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:47.809
If none exists, the system violated linearizability.

546
00:46:49.159 --> 00:46:50.290
Question 11.

547
00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:54.709
How does Jepsen find consistency bugs that survive normal testing?

548
00:46:54.989 --> 00:46:56.354
Thirty-second answer:

549
00:46:56.634 --> 00:47:14.042
Jepsen drives a real distributed database with randomized concurrent operations while a nemesis injects real faults, network partitions, process pauses, clock skew, records the exact real-time history, and checks it with a linearizability or anomaly checker.

550
00:47:14.322 --> 00:47:23.389
Normal tests never combine concurrency with faults with an automated consistency oracle, so they miss the bugs that only appear in that combination.

551
00:47:24.739 --> 00:47:25.827
Question 12.

552
00:47:26.107 --> 00:47:30.309
How do you test the resilience patterns (timeout, retry, circuit breaker)?

553
00:47:30.589 --> 00:47:31.955
Thirty-second answer:

554
00:47:32.235 --> 00:47:49.067
With fault injection: make the dependency slow, error, or unavailable and assert the pattern behaves, the timeout fires at the bound, retries are idempotent and capped with backoff+jitter, the breaker opens after the threshold and short-circuits, and a fallback is served.

555
00:47:49.347 --> 00:47:55.960
You test these against a controllable fake dependency (or a real one with a fault switch), not the happy path.

556
00:47:57.310 --> 00:47:58.590
Question 13.

557
00:47:58.870 --> 00:48:02.838
A field in a provider's response is used by three consumers.

558
00:48:03.118 --> 00:48:04.633
How do you change it safely?

559
00:48:04.913 --> 00:48:06.278
Thirty-second answer:

560
00:48:06.558 --> 00:48:07.795
Look at the contracts.

561
00:48:08.075 --> 00:48:25.633
If the change touches a field a consumer's contract relies on, the provider's verification fails and you don't ship until the consumers are updated, you evolve additively (add the new field, keep the old), migrate consumers, then remove the old field once no contract references it.

562
00:48:25.913 --> 00:48:29.049
If the field is in no contract, you can change it freely.

563
00:48:30.399 --> 00:48:31.593
Question 14.

564
00:48:31.873 --> 00:48:35.713
How do you design tests to run fast enough that people keep them green?

565
00:48:35.993 --> 00:48:37.359
Thirty-second answer:

566
00:48:37.639 --> 00:48:42.097
Keep the pyramid: most tests unit or property (parallel, milliseconds).

567
00:48:42.377 --> 00:48:46.196
Make cross-service confidence come from contracts, not e2e.

568
00:48:46.476 --> 00:48:55.713
For integration, reuse a single real dependency with rollback isolation instead of a fresh container per test, and shard or parallelize.

569
00:48:55.993 --> 00:49:01.007
Run the fast layers on every push (10 min) and the slow ones pre-merge or nightly.

570
00:49:01.287 --> 00:49:05.041
A slow suite gets bypassed, so speed is a correctness property.

571
00:49:06.391 --> 00:49:07.287
Architect.

572
00:49:07.567 --> 00:49:08.762
Question 15.

573
00:49:09.042 --> 00:49:13.607
Design a testing strategy for a payments-critical microservices platform.

574
00:49:13.887 --> 00:49:15.253
Thirty-second answer:

575
00:49:15.533 --> 00:49:17.559
Pyramid with a strong contract layer.

576
00:49:17.839 --> 00:49:25.818
Unit + property tests for all money or serialization or idempotency logic (money conservation as a property).

577
00:49:26.098 --> 00:49:29.682
Consumer-driven contracts gating every cross-service deploy.

578
00:49:29.962 --> 00:49:33.589
Integration tests on real datastores with fault injection.

579
00:49:33.869 --> 00:49:42.210
Jepsen or linearizability checks for the ledger and inventory (anything claiming strong consistency), run nightly under a nemesis.

580
00:49:42.490 --> 00:49:44.474
Mutation score as the merge gate.

581
00:49:44.754 --> 00:49:48.637
Canary or synthetic journeys and SLO monitoring in prod.

582
00:49:48.917 --> 00:49:54.037
Invest proportionally to risk, payments get everything, static content gets the base.

583
00:49:55.387 --> 00:49:56.581
Question 16.

584
00:49:56.861 --> 00:50:00.595
What is deterministic simulation testing and when is it worth it?

585
00:50:00.875 --> 00:50:02.240
Thirty-second answer:

586
00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:13.165
You run the entire system, nodes, network, clock, disk, on a single-threaded deterministic simulator driven by a seed, so every run is exactly reproducible.

587
00:50:13.445 --> 00:50:22.064
You inject partitions, reorderings, crashes and clock skew and explore millions of schedules; a failing seed replays the exact bug.

588
00:50:22.344 --> 00:50:25.309
It turns Heisenbugs into reproducible failures.

589
00:50:25.589 --> 00:50:36.213
It's worth it for the implementation of core distributed infrastructure (a database, a consensus system), where correctness under adversarial scheduling is existential.

590
00:50:37.563 --> 00:50:38.907
Question 17.

591
00:50:39.187 --> 00:50:43.987
How do you test data migrations and schema changes safely (Ch 28)?

592
00:50:44.267 --> 00:50:45.633
Thirty-second answer:

593
00:50:45.913 --> 00:51:00.675
Test the migration itself, not just the post-migration code: run it against a realistic-size copy in CI and assert it's online (no long locks), reversible where required, and idempotent or resumable for backfills.

594
00:51:00.955 --> 00:51:10.513
Add contract or compatibility tests so old and new code both work against each intermediate schema, and a reconciliation test for store-to-store moves.

595
00:51:10.793 --> 00:51:16.531
Verify against a real engine, because locking and constraint behaviour are engine-specific.

596
00:51:17.881 --> 00:51:19.055
Question 18.

597
00:51:19.335 --> 00:51:24.348
How do you test time-dependent and eventually-consistent behaviour without flakiness?

598
00:51:24.628 --> 00:51:25.993
Thirty-second answer:

599
00:51:26.273 --> 00:51:27.767
Never use Thread.sleep.

600
00:51:28.047 --> 00:51:34.809
Inject a controllable clock so you can advance time deterministically for TTLs, schedulers, and backoff.

601
00:51:35.089 --> 00:51:44.519
For eventual consistency, await the expected state by polling with a deadline (and assert it converges within a bound), rather than asserting immediately.

602
00:51:44.799 --> 00:51:48.617
For ordering or lag, record and check rather than assume timing.

603
00:51:49.967 --> 00:51:51.205
Question 19.

604
00:51:51.485 --> 00:51:53.895
How much should you test in production, and how?

605
00:51:54.175 --> 00:51:55.541
Thirty-second answer:

606
00:51:55.821 --> 00:51:58.551
As a complement to pre-prod, not a replacement.

607
00:51:58.831 --> 00:52:18.458
Use canary or synthetic monitoring (scripted journeys run continuously against prod), feature-flag canaries and progressive rollouts with automated rollback on SLO regression, shadow or mirror traffic to a new version, and careful chaos experiments in prod once you've earned confidence in staging.

608
00:52:18.738 --> 00:52:25.117
The goal is to catch what only production reveals, real traffic shapes, real data, real dependencies.

609
00:52:26.467 --> 00:52:27.576
Question 20.

610
00:52:27.856 --> 00:52:30.800
Your suite is green but you have no confidence in it.

611
00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:32.061
What do you do?

612
00:52:32.341 --> 00:52:33.707
Thirty-second answer:

613
00:52:33.987 --> 00:52:36.077
Diagnose quality, not quantity.

614
00:52:36.357 --> 00:52:40.923
Run mutation testing to find code that's executed but unverified.

615
00:52:41.203 --> 00:52:49.267
Audit the mocks: is every cross-service boundary backed by a contract test, every datastore by a real-dependency integration test?

616
00:52:49.547 --> 00:52:55.264
Add property tests for the invariant-bearing logic (money, serialization, idempotency).

617
00:52:55.544 --> 00:52:59.192
Add consistency checks for anything claiming strong guarantees.

618
00:52:59.472 --> 00:53:01.179
Kill flakes so red means red.

619
00:53:01.459 --> 00:53:03.080
Then rebalance to the pyramid.

620
00:53:04.430 --> 00:53:05.326
Part 9.

621
00:53:05.606 --> 00:53:07.014
Summary and Practice.

622
00:53:08.264 --> 00:53:09.352
Key takeaways.

623
00:53:09.632 --> 00:53:11.253
Coverage is not correctness.

624
00:53:11.533 --> 00:53:22.115
Measure test quality (mutation testing), and prove properties, contracts, and consistency, not just that code ran (§1.1, §2.11).

625
00:53:22.395 --> 00:53:28.859
Shape the suite as a pyramid with a strong contract layer; avoid the ice-cream cone (§2.2).

626
00:53:29.139 --> 00:53:40.808
Mocks encode assumptions that rot; back every mocked boundary with a contract test and every mocked datastore with real-dependency integration (§2.3, L2, L4).

627
00:53:42.158 --> 00:53:48.793
Property-based tests find the edge cases you didn't think of and shrink them to minimal reproducers (L1).

628
00:53:49.073 --> 00:53:56.177
Consumer-driven contracts let services be tested and deployed independently and catch breaks at the source (L2).

629
00:53:56.457 --> 00:54:03.859
Consistency must be checked, not asserted: record histories and run a linearizability or Jepsen checker (L3).

630
00:54:04.139 --> 00:54:10.155
Integration-test against the real engine with rollback isolation and fault injection (L4).

631
00:54:11.505 --> 00:54:19.953
Kill flakiness (control time, isolate state, await don't sleep) or the suite loses all value (§2.10).

632
00:54:21.303 --> 00:54:22.626
Decision cheat sheet.

633
00:54:22.906 --> 00:54:24.271
Here is a quick table:

634
00:54:24.551 --> 00:54:26.301
Need to test..., and Use.

635
00:54:26.581 --> 00:54:30.805
A pure function or invariant (money, serialization, idempotency):

636
00:54:31.085 --> 00:54:33.026
Property-based test (L1).

637
00:54:33.306 --> 00:54:35.397
Two services agree on an API:

638
00:54:35.677 --> 00:54:37.853
Consumer-driven contract (L2).

639
00:54:38.133 --> 00:54:42.463
Code + real DB or broker behaviour, constraints, transactions:

640
00:54:42.743 --> 00:54:45.559
Integration test on the real engine (L4).

641
00:54:45.839 --> 00:54:49.146
Strong-consistency claim under concurrency + faults:

642
00:54:49.426 --> 00:54:52.199
Linearizability or Jepsen check (L3).

643
00:54:52.479 --> 00:54:53.909
A full revenue journey:

644
00:54:54.189 --> 00:54:57.047
A few end-to-end tests (§2.9).

645
00:54:57.327 --> 00:55:00.506
Resilience patterns (timeout or retry or breaker):

